"[2509.08022v2] MVPBench: A Benchmark and Fine-Tuning Framework for Aligning Large Language Models with Diverse Human Values (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08022v2)\nciteturn3416view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08022v2\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 199\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:  cite58†Image: Cornell University L3: \nL4: cite1†Learn about arXiv becoming an independent nonprofit.†tech.cornell.edu L5: \nL6:  We gratefully acknowledge support from the Simons Foundation, cite2†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , and all contributors. cite3†Donate†info.arxiv.org L7: \nL8: cite59†Image: arxiv logo > cite4†cs > arXiv:2509.08022v2\nL9: \nL10: cite5†Help†info.arxiv.org | cite6†Advanced Search L11:  All fields Title Author Abstract Comments Journal reference ACM classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text\nL12: \nL13: Search\nL14: \nL15: cite60†Image: arXiv logo L16: \nL17: cite7† †www.cornell.edu L18: \nL19: GO\nL20: ## quick links\nL21: \nL22:   * cite8†Login L23:   * cite5†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L24:   * cite9†About†info.arxiv.org L25: \nL26: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL27: \nL28: arXiv:2509.08022v2 (cs)\nL29: \nL30:  This paper has been withdrawn by Yao Liang\nL31: \nL32: [Submitted on 9 Sep 2025 (cite10†v1 ), last revised 16 Sep 2025 (this version, v2)]\nL33: # Title:MVPBench: A Benchmark and Fine-Tuning Framework for Aligning Large Language Models with Diverse Human Values\nL34: \nL35: Authors:cite11†Yao Liang , cite12†Dongcheng Zhao , cite13†Feifei Zhao , cite14†Guobin Shen , cite15†Yuwei Wang , cite16†Dongqi Liang , cite17†Yi Zeng L36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled MVPBench: A Benchmark and Fine-Tuning Framework for Aligning Large Language Models with Diverse Human Values, by Yao Liang and 6 other authors\nL38: \nL39: No PDF available, click to view other formats\nL40: > Abstract:The alignment of large language models (LLMs) with human values is critical for their safe and effective deployment across diverse user populations. However, existing benchmarks often neglect cultural and demographic diversity, leading to limited understanding of how value alignment generalizes globally. In this work, we introduce MVPBench, a novel benchmark that systematically evaluates LLMs' alignment with multi-dimensional human value preferences across 75 countries.\nL41: MVPBench contains 24,020 high-quality instances annotated with fine-grained value labels, personalized questions, and rich demographic metadata, making it the most comprehensive resource of its kind to date. Using MVPBench, we conduct an in-depth analysis of several state-of-the-art LLMs, revealing substantial disparities in alignment performance across geographic and demographic lines.\nL42: We further demonstrate that lightweight fine-tuning methods, such as Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), can significantly enhance value alignment in both in-domain and out-of-domain settings. Our findings underscore the necessity for population-aware alignment evaluation and provide actionable insights for building culturally adaptive and value-sensitive LLMs.\nL43: MVPBench serves as a practical foundation for future research on global alignment, personalized value modeling, and equitable AI development.\nL44: Comments: Some parts of the paper need to be revised. We would therefore like to withdraw the paper and resubmit it after making the necessary changes\nL45: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL46: Cite as: cite18†arXiv:2509.08022 [cs.CL]\nL47:   (or  cite19†arXiv:2509.08022v2 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL48:   cite20†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.08022†doi.org L49: \nL50: Focus to learn more\nL51: \nL52:   arXiv-issued DOI via 